[13.525065, "i", "bash /opt/gnomon-run.sh 'Train a LoRA (Low-Rank Adaptation) adapter for a Hugging Face language model to perform biomedical question answering.\n\nYou are provided with a biomedical multiple-choice QA dataset in the /app/data directory containing:\n- train.json: 300 training samples\n- val.json: 100 validation samples\n\nNOTE: The test set is NOT available in the data folder during training to prevent overfitting. \nIt is stored separately and only accessible during the evaluation phase.\n\nEach sample has the following structure:\n{\n  \"question\": \"What is the primary function of hemoglobin?\",\n  \"options\": [\"A) Transport oxygen\", \"B) Fight infections\", \"C) Clot blood\", \"D) Digest food\"],  // Exactly 4 options (A, B, C, D)\n  \"answer\": \"A\"\n}\n\nRequirements:\n1. Use the openai-community/gpt2 model as the base model for fine-tuning\n2. Implement LoRA fine-tuning with the following configuration:\n   - LoRA rank (r): 8\n   - LoRA alpha: 16\n   - Target modules: ['\"'\"'c_attn'\"'\"'] (attention layers)\n3. Train the model using the pr"]
[13.525193, "i", "ovided training and validation sets\n4. Save your training script to /app/train_lora.py for verification\n5. Save the trained LoRA adapter to /app/experiments/lora_adapter, including:\n   - adapter_config.json (LoRA configuration)\n   - adapter_model.safetensors (LoRA weights)\n   - tokenizer_config.json (tokenizer configuration)\n   - adapter_config should include default arguments including '\"'\"'base_model_name_or_path'\"'\"' and '\"'\"'task_type'\"'\"' \n6. It is recommended to use the PEFT (peft==0.15.0) library for implementing LoRA fine-tuning.\n7. Create a results file at /app/lora_results.json with the exact schema below\n\nRequired /app/lora_results.json schema:\n{\n  \"model\": string,           // Base model name used (e.g., \"openai-community/gpt2\")\n  \"lora_config\": {\n    \"r\": number,            // LoRA rank (must be 8)\n    \"alpha\": number,        // LoRA alpha (must be 16)\n    \"epochs\": number        // Number of training epochs\n  },\n  \"training_samples\": number,     // Number of training samples used\n  \"validation_s"]
[13.52557, "i", "amples\": number    // Number of validation samples used\n}\n\nExpected outputs:\n- /app/train_lora.py: Your training script\n- /app/experiments/lora_adapter/: Directory containing the LoRA adapter weights and configuration, and can be loaded to generate responses\n- /app/lora_results.json: JSON file matching the schema above\n\nPerformance target: The fine-tuned model should achieve >35% accuracy on the held-out biomedical QA test set.'; tmux wait -S done\r"]
[13.525616, "o", "bash /opt/gnomon-run.sh 'Train a LoRA (Low-Rank Adaptation) adapter for a Hugging Face language model to perform biomedical question answering.\r\n\u001b[?2004l\r\u001b[?2004h> \r\n\u001b[?2004l\r"]
[13.526873, "o", "\u001b[?2004h> You are provided with a biomedical multiple-choice QA dataset in the /app/data directory containing:\r\n\u001b[?2004l\r\u001b[?2004h> - train.json: 300 training samples\r\n\u001b[?2004l\r\u001b[?2004h> - val.json: 100 validation samples\r\n\u001b[?2004l\r\u001b[?2004h> \r\n\u001b[?2004l\r\u001b[?2004h> NOTE: The test set is NOT available in the data folder during training to prevent overfitting. \r\n\u001b[?2004l\r\u001b[?2004h> It is stored separately and only accessible during the evaluation phase.\r\n\u001b[?2004l\r\u001b[?2004h> \r\n\u001b[?2004l\r\u001b[?2004h> Each sample has the following structure:\r\n\u001b[?2004l\r\u001b[?2004h> {\r\n\u001b[?2004l\r\u001b[?2004h>   \"question\": \"What is the primary function of hemoglobin?\",\r\n\u001b[?2004l\r\u001b[?2004h>   \"options\": [\"A) Transport oxygen\", \"B) Fight infections\", \"C) Clot blood\", \"D) Digest food\"],  // Exactly 4 options (A, B, C, D)\r\n\u001b[?2004l\r\u001b[?2004h>   \"answer\": \"A\"\r\n\u001b[?2004l\r\u001b[?2004h> }\r\n\u001b[?2004l\r\u001b[?2004h> \r\n\u001b[?2004l\r\u001b[?2004h> Requirements:\r\n\u001b[?2004l\r\u001b[?2004h> 1. Use the openai-community/gpt2 model as the base model for fine-tuning\r\n\u001b[?2004l\r\u001b[?2004h> 2. Implemen"]
[13.52815, "o", "t LoRA fine-tuning with the following configuration:\r\n\u001b[?2004l\r\u001b[?2004h>    - LoRA rank (r): 8\r\n\u001b[?2004l\r\u001b[?2004h>    - LoRA alpha: 16\r\n\u001b[?2004l\r\u001b[?2004h>    - Target modules: ['\"'\"'c_attn'\"'\"'] (attention layers)\r\n\u001b[?2004l\r\u001b[?2004h> 3. Train the model using the provided training and validation sets\r\n\u001b[?2004l\r\u001b[?2004h> 4. Save your training script to /app/train_lora.py for verification\r\n\u001b[?2004l\r\u001b[?2004h> 5. Save the trained LoRA adapter to /app/experiments/lora_adapter, including:\r\n\u001b[?2004l\r\u001b[?2004h>    - adapter_config.json (LoRA configuration)\r\n\u001b[?2004l\r\u001b[?2004h>    - adapter_model.safetensors (LoRA weights)\r\n\u001b[?2004l\r\u001b[?2004h>    - tokenizer_config.json (tokenizer configuration)\r\n\u001b[?2004l\r\u001b[?2004h>    - adapter_config should include default arguments including '\"'\"'base_model_name_or_path'\"'\"' and '\"'\"'task_type'\"'\"' \r\n\u001b[?2004l\r\u001b[?2004h> 6. It is recommended to use the PEFT (peft==0.15.0) library for implementing LoRA fine-tuning.\r\n\u001b[?2004l\r\u001b[?2004h> "]
[13.528203, "o", "7. Create a results file at /app/lora_results.json with the exact schema below\r\n\u001b[?2004l\r"]
